Musicolet Main Interface: Architectural Blueprint
1. The Master Layout (UI Hierarchy)
The interface is divided into three fixed zones that never disappear, ensuring zero-latency navigation.

Zone A: The Navigation Header (Top 10%)

Structure: A scrollable horizontal tab bar.

Total Tabs: [Main Player] | [Queues] | [Folders] | [Albums] | [Artists] | [Playlists] | [Genres] | [Songs].

Behavior: Tapping a tab instantly swaps the central content (Zone B). There are no transition animations to keep it feeling "instant."
There are also configuration to decide which tabs exist on the master layout. (if too much tabs is shown and cause UI breach, shorten them into icons only)

Zone B: The Dynamic Content Area (Middle 75%)

This area changes based on the selected tab (e.g., showing a grid of albums or a list of folders).

Zone C: The persistent Mini-Player (Bottom 15%)

A slim bar containing the current song title, artist, play/pause button, and a thin "seek bar" at the very top of the bar.

2. The "Now Playing" Screen (Detailed Component Map)
When the user taps the [Main Player] tab or swipes up on the mini-player, they see this high-density dashboard.

Visual Elements
Central Artwork: A large square album cover.

Interaction: Tapping the artwork toggles Lyrics Overlay. Long-pressing allows the user to change/edit the album art.

Dynamic Background: A blurred, desaturated version of the current album art that fills the entire screen background.

Functional Controls (The "Power User" Row)
Below the album art, Musicolet places 4–5 small icon buttons for quick access:

Queue Icon: Opens the list of upcoming songs.

A-B Repeat: Allows the user to select a specific segment of a song to loop.

Playback Speed: A shortcut to a slider (0.5x to 5.0x).

Sleep Timer: A clock icon that shows remaining time or number of songs.

Tag Editor: A "pencil" icon to edit the metadata of the current song immediately.

3. The "Queues" Tab (Multi-Queue Management)
This is the core differentiator. Your agent must implement this specific logic:

Tab Layout: A vertical list of the current queue's songs.

Queue Switcher: At the top of this tab, there is a small "1/20" indicator. Tapping it opens a grid where the user can switch between up to 20 different saved queues.

Live Status: Each queue "remembers" its own shuffle/repeat state and the exact millisecond where it was last paused.

4. Library Browsing (Folders & Lists)
Fast-Scroll Handle: When scrolling long lists (like [Songs] or [Artists]), a handle appears on the right with the alphabet (A-Z) for instant jumping.

Multi-Select Engine: * Long-pressing any item triggers "Batch Mode."

A bottom toolbar appears with options: [Play Next] | [Enqueue] | [Add to Playlist] | [Delete].

Folder View Toggle: Inside the [Folders] tab, there is a toggle for:

Linear: Flat list of all folders with music.

Hierarchical: Navigating through sub-folders (Folder > Sub-folder > Song).

5. Global Elements (Settings & Search)
The Three-Dot Menu (Top Right):

Contains: [Equalizer], [Sleep Timer], [Settings], [Close App].

Search Bar:

Unlike other apps, Musicolet’s search is "context-aware." If you are in the [Artists] tab and tap search, it prioritizes artists. If you tap the global search, it searches the entire device storage.

Agent Implementation Checklist
No Navigation Drawer: Ensure the agent does not build a hamburger menu; use the horizontal top tabs instead.

State Management: The "Mini-Player" must be a global state so it doesn't "reset" when switching between library tabs.

UI Density: Use a 12pt or 14pt font for song titles to fit more information on the screen.

Custom Icons: Prioritize functional icons (Speed, Timer) on the main screen over "clean" whitespace.

Important: all these layouts are highly customizable, leave space for further implementations
